Score the relocation ADDEND on relocation types that store it inline.

objdiff renders a relocated operand as the target SYMBOL and compares that, on the
grounds that the linked-address bytes are meaningless before the link. On x86 COFF
that reasoning has a hole: `IMAGE_REL_I386_DIR32` has no addend field, so the addend
lives INSIDE the instruction's displacement - the very field the diff masks. objdiff
already recovers it (`Arch::relocation_override` reads the 4 bytes at the reloc site
into `Relocation::addend`), and `FunctionRelocDiffs::{All,NameAddress}` do compare it
via `symbol_name_addend_matches` / `address_eq`. But `FunctionRelocDiffs::DataValue`
short-circuits that whole clause - it was meant to relax the NAME/ADDRESS identity in
favour of comparing the pointed-to value, and dropped the addend along with it. So
under `data_value`

    mov word ptr [esi + 0x20000], dx    ; reloc -> ?g_clut@@3PAEA
    mov word ptr [esi + 0x1fffe], dx    ; reloc -> ?g_clut@@3PAEA

score identical: same symbol, and `display_ins_data_literals` returns nothing for a
`.bss` target, so the value test is vacuous too. That is the only operand class where
a wrong constant is free, and it hid a live off-by-one over a 3 x 32768-entry LUT.

The fix compares the addend when BOTH sides resolve to the same symbol and the
relocation type's addend is a plain symbol-relative offset. Both restrictions matter:

  * type - `REL32` (and the AMD64 `REL32_n` family) store a SITE-relative value, so
    two objects legitimately hold different bytes for the same call. The predicate is
    a new `Arch` method defaulting to `false`, so no architecture changes behaviour
    until it opts in; only x86 (COFF DIR32/DIR32NB, ELF R_386_32) does here.
  * same symbol - when the two sides name DIFFERENT symbols the addends are not
    commensurable. A delinker routinely names an interior address after a
    neighbouring global, which shifts the addend without changing the referenced
    byte; that case is left to the existing name/address/value tests.

Upstream does not do this because `data_value` exists for projects whose target
objects are produced by a linker-splitting tool with unreliable symbol names, where
the pointed-to VALUE is the trustworthy signal; nobody had noticed that the addend is
a third, independent signal that survives unreliable names. Drop this patch once
objdiff compares reloc addends under `data_value` upstream (or grows a separate
`addend` strictness knob).

2026-08-13: the project now runs `functionRelocDiffs = all` (gate_selftest asserts
it), which already requires name+addend OR `address_eq` - so this patch's remaining
live effect under `all` is killing the `address_eq` rescue for a SAME-named symbol
with a differing addend. That corner is real here, not theoretical: base and
delinked-target objects both use small 0-based section-relative symbol addresses
(commensurable spaces), and the delinker's per-symbol packing places a same-named
referent at different offsets, so a wrong addend can be exactly compensated. Keep
the patch under either mode; it is the strictest-mode ruling in patch form.

